we are in the main gallery of the Albert  and Shirley small special collections  library at the University of Virginia  our current exhibition is Faulkner life  and works this is a large exhibition  surveying the Magnificent William  Faulkner collections that we have here  at the library William Faulkner was a  great American novelist who was born in  Mississippi and spent the last few years  of his career in the late 1950s and  early 1960s at UVA he's best known for  his novels the sound in the fury as a  lead eyeing sanctuary Absalom Absalom  and he was also a poet and a short story  writer well it's been quite a long time  since our last monumental Faulkner  exhibition even though we often bring  individual items out or put them on  display in other contexts we haven't  really told the full Faulkner story and  given the public access to a huge range  of materials in the collection for quite  some time it also is the 60th  anniversary of his arrival here at the  University as writer in residence and we  still have people coming to visit who  tell us about having met Faulkner while  they were here while Faulkner was at UVA  he was working on his own writing he  also spent a lot of time here in formal  and informal events meeting with  students faculty community members  faculty wives female students from local  or nearby women's colleges since UVA was  only men at the time and other groups to  talk about his novels about the state of  literature today and pretty much  anything else that they asked him about  he also really enjoyed living in  Virginia he had always had a dream to  learn to fox hunt and he learned to fox  hunt while he was here and joined the  farmington hunt and would go fox hunting  on the weekends whenever he possibly  could we have a lot of wonderful  artifacts of course from Faulkner's time  at UVA many that have come from UVA  sources among other things we have the  typewriter that he was issued by the  University even with the University  property Stan  on the back we have a jacket that he  wore he as you can see when you look at  the jacket it's it's pretty torn up and  and ratty he liked to keep his clothes  for a long time he left this jacket  hanging in his office when he went on  his last trip to Oxford Mississippi when  he passed away and in the pocket of the  jacket was a pipe and some pipe cleaners  and we even put the pipe cleaners on  display because we just thought visitors  would enjoy that close connection to  Faulkner the collections are so vast it  was really difficult to decide how to  tell the story of Faulkner's lifetime so  what we decided to do was look at the  various personae that were constructed  either by Faulkner actively or by the  circumstances in which he found himself  throughout his life and structured the  episode around those so there are 13  different aspects of his personality  displayed we tried to cover the very  personal the professional and the  lesser-known for instance I don't think  very many people know that Faulkner was  a wonderful artist and he drew all the  way through his life a lot of people  also don't know that he did really  interesting work after he won the Nobel  as a sort of literary ambassador working  for the US State Department so we tried  to pull out some unexpected stories and  also show some of the most iconic items  in the collection some materials that  demonstrate Faulkner's family history  are also on display and one that I find  incredibly interesting and poignant and  that we placed front and center at the  beginning of the exhibition is actually  from long before Faulkner was born it is  the receipt for a slave sold by  Faulkner's grandfather we thought it was  really important to put that in the  exhibition to point out just how  important that history was to how  Faulkner developed as a writer the issue  of slavery the issue of race relations  in the south these are central to his  work and are revered as topics covered  with brilliance and care throughout his  work so we really wanted to make sure  that  we put that really interesting artifact  on display as a symbol of this problem  that he inherited one of the most  interesting episodes to me of Faulkner's  life is when he spent time in Hollywood  as a screenwriter  now Faulkner would not agree with me he  didn't particularly like his time in  Hollywood it was a job that he took  writing screenplays for the studio's to  make money he was having a very hard  time making ends meet because his  writing was very difficult his books  were not selling as well as they might  have if they were more readable so he  needed to find other ways to support his  family screenwriting worked for him for  a long time he did screenwriting work  off and on for a couple of decades the  items on display are really quite  wonderful probably my favorite item is a  fragment of his screenplay for the film  The Big Sleep  which is of course considered a  masterpiece of film for its time in one  of his essays in the 1950s Faulkner  refers to himself as having the position  of a white southerner so we took that  that said what is this white southerner  figure that Faulkner inhabits anyone who  approaches Faulkner's works sees him  engaging with the problem of race and he  saw himself very much as having a  challenging position in relation  especially to the african-american  community around Oxford and members of  that community feature in fictional form  throughout his works the section of the  exhibition called white southerner looks  in particular at how Faulkner grappled  with questions of racial identity in the  South in the 1950s he wins the Nobel  Prize in 1950 and from that point on his  fame just explodes his books are sold in  massive quantities and he's known he  becomes sort of a you know a household  name and at the same time the civil  rights movement is starting to really  gather steam and the debates over  integration become more and more vocal  and more and more polarizing in the  United States so Faulkner begins to be  called upon as a public figure to  comment upon integration  he's even asked when he's abroad in  foreign countries  as an American as a southern American  tell us what you think about this issue  because of course all over the world  issues having to do with integration are  being reported we have a lot of  documents in the collection that show  Faulkner trying really hard to figure  out how to determine his own position on  integration what's wonderful about these  documents as they show that Faulkner  found himself sort of stuck between  different positions on the one hand his  view that integration needed to happen  put him at odds with a lot of whites in  Mississippi  however he often called for a slow  gradual asst move towards integration  which put him at odds with northerners  he also felt that he had a loyalty to a  south so he didn't want the north to  tell the South what to do so he's  constantly grappling and shifting his  perspective over time as an intellectual  of his moment his perspective is really  unusual and really interesting and I  found that visitors to the exhibition  really find a lot to think about in that  section and I have to say that's  probably where I learned the most in  curating the exhibition we want all  audiences to enjoy this exhibition so if  you're a Faulkner fanatic you'll be able  to commune with the manuscripts of works  and if you are not a Faulkner fanatic if  you maybe even had some trauma with  Faulkner in high school which some  people have told me they did we want you  to be able to understand his biography  because his life story is really  interesting I think we want people to  come away with a sense of Faulkner as an  entire person the whole person and all  of the aspects of his life story that  influenced his ability to sit down and  write these masterpieces so much of his  time was spent struggling to make money  struggling to care for his family it's  really remarkable to see what he  accomplished and we also want people to  get a sense of the full range of  material that can be found in a literary  archive it's a it's a real treasure and  we're so happy to be able to share it
 

